<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adapted from FOPP "Infinite Loops" (More About Iteration). GNU FDL 1.3; see ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-infinite-loops">
  <title>&#x1F469;&#x200D;&#x1F4BB; Infinite Loops</title>

  <p>
    A loop stops when its condition becomes <c>false</c>.
    So what if the condition never becomes <c>false</c>?
    Then the loop never stops.
    This is called an <term>infinite loop</term>, and it is one of the most common mistakes in programming.
  </p>

  <p>
    Look at this loop. <alert>Do not run it.</alert>
  </p>

  <program language="javascript">
    <code>
for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 20; i--) {
  console.log(i);
}
    </code>
  </program>

  <p>
    Trace it: <c>i</c> starts at 1. The condition <c>1 &lt;= 20</c> is <c>true</c>.
    The update <c>i--</c> makes <c>i</c> 0. <c>0 &lt;= 20</c> is <c>true</c>. Then -1, then -2...
    The update moves <c>i</c> <em>away</em> from the stop number, so <c>i &lt;= 20</c> stays <c>true</c> forever.
  </p>

  <p>
    To spot an infinite loop, ask one question:
    <alert>Does the update move the loop variable toward the stop, or away from it?</alert>
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><p>Counting up (<c>&lt;</c> or <c>&lt;=</c>) needs an update that makes <c>i</c> bigger, like <c>i++</c>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Counting down (<c>&gt;</c> or <c>&gt;=</c>) needs an update that makes <c>i</c> smaller, like <c>i--</c>.</p></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If the update and the condition do not match, the loop runs forever.
  </p>

  <note>
    <title>If you do run an infinite loop</title>
    <p>
      The browser tab will freeze, because it is busy running your loop and has no time for anything else.
      Close the tab or reload the page, then fix the loop before you run it again.
      The code boxes in this book save your work, so you will not lose it.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p><alert>Check your understanding</alert></p>

  <exercise xml:id="mc-infinite-which" label="mc-infinite-which">
    <statement>
      <p>What happens when this loop runs?</p>
      <program language="javascript">
        <code>
for (let i = 1; i &gt; 0; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
        </code>
      </program>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>It never stops.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Right. <c>i</c> only gets bigger, so <c>i &gt; 0</c> is always <c>true</c>.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>It prints 1, then stops.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. After the first pass, <c>i</c> is 2, and <c>2 &gt; 0</c> is still <c>true</c>.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>It prints nothing.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. <c>1 &gt; 0</c> is <c>true</c> at the first check, so the body runs.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>It counts down from 1 to 0, then stops.</p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. <c>i++</c> makes <c>i</c> bigger, not smaller.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="mc-infinite-fix" label="mc-infinite-fix">
    <statement>
      <p>This loop is meant to count down from 10 to 1, but it never stops. Which change fixes it?</p>
      <program language="javascript">
        <code>
for (let i = 10; i &gt;= 1; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}
        </code>
      </program>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Change <c>i++</c> to <c>i--</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>Right. Now <c>i</c> moves toward 1, and the loop stops after 1.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Change <c>i &gt;= 1</c> to <c>i &lt;= 1</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. Then <c>10 &lt;= 1</c> is <c>false</c> at the first check, so nothing prints.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Change <c>let i = 10</c> to <c>let i = 1</c></p></statement>
        <feedback><p>No. <c>i</c> would still get bigger forever.</p></feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
</section>
